{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/a2-cafe-language-exchange-meetup/audio/main-747cc474e5a3.mp3","durationSec":293.784,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":4.12,"text":"A language exchange is a trade: you give something, and you get something."},{"start":4.78,"end":7.7,"text":"You help someone with your language, and they help you with theirs."},{"start":8.68,"end":12,"text":"For example, you speak Japanese and want to practise English."},{"start":12.9,"end":16.14,"text":"Then you look for an English speaker who wants to practise Japanese."},{"start":17.42,"end":20.26,"text":"Many language cafés split the time in half."},{"start":21.02,"end":23.04,"text":"You talk in one language for half an hour."},{"start":23.74,"end":26.24,"text":"Then you talk in the other language for half an hour."},{"start":26.78,"end":29.84,"text":"This way, both people get the same time to learn."},{"start":31.04,"end":35.24,"text":"In this lesson, you visit the Dutch Language Café in The Hague."},{"start":35.24,"end":36.8,"text":"The Hague is a city in the Netherlands."},{"start":37.6,"end":41.44,"text":"On Thursday evenings, its language exchange starts at 19:30."},{"start":42.28,"end":46.34,"text":"When you arrive, you tell the host: I'm here for the language exchange."},{"start":47.12,"end":49.52,"text":"But how do you find the right person in a busy room?"},{"start":50.3,"end":52.54,"text":"The café gives you a language sticker."},{"start":52.94,"end":55.12,"text":"You write your languages on it and wear it."},{"start":55.57,"end":59.7,"text":"You write your native language and the languages you want to practise."},{"start":59.7,"end":63.04,"text":"Your native language is the first language you learned as a child."},{"start":63.9,"end":67.08,"text":"Other people look at your sticker and know your languages at once."},{"start":67.88,"end":70.96,"text":"They often ask too: Which languages do you speak?"},{"start":71.86,"end":74.86,"text":"You answer: My native language is Japanese."},{"start":76,"end":78.92,"text":"At 20:00, the speed exchange starts."},{"start":79.62,"end":81.9,"text":"You talk with one person for 3 minutes."},{"start":82.78,"end":84.26,"text":"Then you move to the next person."},{"start":84.26,"end":85.56,"text":"Why so short?"},{"start":86.36,"end":88.28,"text":"This way, you meet many people quickly."},{"start":89.02,"end":92.7,"text":"Later, everyone sits at language tables, for example an English table."},{"start":93.32,"end":96.7,"text":"At the Dutch Language Café, the Thursday ticket costs €11."},{"start":97.88,"end":99.84,"text":"The ticket pays for snacks and games."},{"start":100.68,"end":103.7,"text":"The snacks are things like popcorn and nuts."},{"start":104.44,"end":106.82,"text":"You ask the host: Are the snacks included?"},{"start":107.34,"end":108.72,"text":"The host says yes."},{"start":109.28,"end":112.22,"text":"But drinks are not in the ticket, so you buy them at the bar."},{"start":112.74,"end":116.88,"text":"On the menu, you see a funny name: koffie verkeerd."},{"start":116.88,"end":119.54,"text":"In English, koffie verkeerd is wrong coffee."},{"start":120.02,"end":120.5,"text":"Why wrong?"},{"start":121.12,"end":123.5,"text":"A normal Dutch coffee has only a little milk."},{"start":123.5,"end":128.06,"text":"Koffie verkeerd has more milk than coffee, so it is the wrong way around."},{"start":128.86,"end":131.96,"text":"At the bar, you order: Can I have a koffie verkeerd, please?"},{"start":132.7,"end":134.58,"text":"Then you ask: How much is it?"},{"start":134.9,"end":136.96,"text":"It costs €4.00."},{"start":136.96,"end":140.18,"text":"If you are hungry, you ask the waiter: What is an empanada?"},{"start":140.18,"end":144.72,"text":"It is a small pastry, with cheese, meat or vegetables inside."},{"start":145.4,"end":148.16,"text":"An empanada costs €6.50."},{"start":149.02,"end":151.84,"text":"In the Netherlands, people shake hands when they meet someone new."},{"start":152.66,"end":155.36,"text":"At the same time, they say their name: Hi!"},{"start":155.7,"end":157.18,"text":"I'm Sanne."},{"start":157.18,"end":158.92,"text":"Often they say only their first name."},{"start":159.6,"end":163.78,"text":"In Japan, many people bow, or bend forward, and say their family name."},{"start":164.4,"end":168.12,"text":"But for Dutch people, a hello without a name is rude, or not polite."},{"start":168.9,"end":169.24,"text":"Why?"},{"start":169.8,"end":172.88,"text":"Dutch people like to be clear, so they start with a name."},{"start":173.6,"end":176.1,"text":"When you hear a new name, you say your name too."},{"start":176.58,"end":178.36,"text":"Then you say: Nice to meet you."},{"start":179.24,"end":182.64,"text":"The first time you meet a Dutch person, do not ask: How are you?"},{"start":183.56,"end":187.38,"text":"Many Dutch people use this question only with people they already know."},{"start":188.3,"end":190.2,"text":"A stranger is a person you do not know."},{"start":190.8,"end":194.36,"text":"If a stranger asks this, a Dutch person may think you met before."},{"start":194.98,"end":196.28,"text":"They may think they forgot you!"},{"start":197,"end":200.06,"text":"In a 3-minute speed exchange, you do not have much time."},{"start":200.06,"end":205.86,"text":"So say three things: your name, your city and why you study the language."},{"start":205.86,"end":209.6,"text":"Lucía from Spain says: Hi!"},{"start":209.6,"end":210.62,"text":"I'm Lucía."},{"start":210.62,"end":211.4,"text":"I'm from Valencia."},{"start":211.94,"end":213.82,"text":"I study Dutch because I work in The Hague."},{"start":214.84,"end":216.54,"text":"The last part is the most useful."},{"start":216.86,"end":220.46,"text":"It gives people a question to ask, for example: Where do you work?"},{"start":221.4,"end":223.62,"text":"Then Lucía asks: What about you?"},{"start":224.16,"end":228.22,"text":"The best tool for a long conversation is the follow-up question."},{"start":229.02,"end":231.66,"text":"This is a question about what the other person just said."},{"start":231.66,"end":234.68,"text":"Lucía says: I work at a hotel."},{"start":235.06,"end":238.1,"text":"A new question jumps to a new idea: Do you like football?"},{"start":238.98,"end":242.5,"text":"A follow-up question stays with her idea: What do you do at the hotel?"},{"start":242.5,"end":245.96,"text":"Lucía also says: I moved here from Valencia."},{"start":246.94,"end":249.7,"text":"Each follow-up question uses one part of what she says."},{"start":250.48,"end":252.52,"text":"You ask about her reason: Really?"},{"start":252.52,"end":254.2,"text":"Why did you move here?"},{"start":254.96,"end":257.32,"text":"You ask about her city: What is Valencia like?"},{"start":258.06,"end":260.86,"text":"You ask about time: How long have you lived here?"},{"start":262.3,"end":266.86,"text":"In 2017, scientists at Harvard University studied how strangers talk."},{"start":267.52,"end":270.56,"text":"People who asked more questions were liked more by their partners."},{"start":270.56,"end":273.5,"text":"Follow-up questions worked best of all."},{"start":273.5,"end":275.16,"text":"Why?"},{"start":275.16,"end":277.92,"text":"A follow-up question shows that you are really listening."},{"start":278.84,"end":281.26,"text":"At 21:30, the exchange ends."},{"start":281.76,"end":285.1,"text":"When you want to leave, end the conversation in a kind way."},{"start":285.86,"end":286.92,"text":"Do not just walk away."},{"start":287.56,"end":289.16,"text":"The other person gave you their time."},{"start":289.88,"end":293.38,"text":"So say thank you with kind words: It was nice to talk to you."}],"words":[{"text":"A","start":0,"end":0.18},{"text":"language","start":0.18,"end":0.44},{"text":"exchange","start":0.44,"end":0.92},{"text":"is","start":0.92,"end":1.2},{"text":"a","start":1.2,"end":1.34},{"text":"trade","start":1.34,"end":1.64},{"text":"you","start":2.12,"end":2.2},{"text":"give","start":2.2,"end":